## Your shop works. Until you look at what it costs.

Shop tab set up. Products listed. Orders in Messenger. By every visible measure, your Facebook Shop works. So why does growth feel stuck? Same follower count for months. Same faces ordering. New customers never finding you first.

Nothing is broken. That's the problem. A Facebook Shop handles the visible 20% of selling and quietly skips the rest: Google visibility, stranger trust, ownership of anything. Here's the bill.

## Facebook Shop vs a website: what's actually different

Side by side, the gap stops being abstract:

| You need...                  | Facebook Shop | Your own website |
| ---------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------- |
| Customers find you on Google | No            | Yes              |
| Product pages you control    | Within limits | Fully            |
| Checkout that fits your flow | Facebook's way| Your way         |
| Customer list you own        | No            | Yes              |
| Business email address       | Impossible    | Yes              |
| Survives a page restriction  | No            | Yes              |

Every "no" is a ceiling. Walk through the ones that hurt most.

## Strangers can't find you

Regulars know your page name. New buyers don't. They Google "buy panjabi online" or "best cake shop Uttara." A Facebook Shop is nearly invisible there. Google ranks websites, not shop tabs. So every search in your category sends buyers to competitors with sites. You wait for the algorithm to show your posts.

Quietest leak there is. Orders you never knew existed, going to whoever owns the result.

## Your customers aren't really yours

Followers, Messenger threads, order history. All of it lives inside Facebook's system. You can't take the customer list with you. Can't email past buyers about a new collection. And if the page gets restricted (wrong flag, hacked admin, policy mix-up), the whole relationship pauses until Facebook responds.

A website flips ownership. Your domain. Your buyer list. Your order history. The page keeps bringing discovery while the business sits somewhere nobody can throttle.

## Checkout friction and the trust gap

Facebook checkout is one-size-fits-all. Serious buyers notice what's missing. No proper return policy page. No professional invoice email. Confirmations from a personal Gmail address. Each one is a small doubt. Doubts stack up exactly where they cost most: payment time.

The email part stings twice. No `orders@yourbusiness.com` without a domain. And a domain is the same purchase a website starts with. (More here: [why your business needs email on its own domain](/blog/why-business-email).)

## What to do about it (no technical skills needed)

Don't abandon the shop. Give it a home base:

1. **Get your domain.** `yourbusiness.com`. Cheap, yearly. Everything hangs off it.
2. **Put up a simple website.** Products, prices, how to order, how to reach you. One clear page Google can find and buyers can trust. Link it from the shop, the page button, every pinned post.
3. **Add business email on that domain.** Confirmations and invoices from your business name. Never a Gmail address again.

Then sell on Facebook exactly like before. Except now every post, boost, and Messenger chat points somewhere you own. Reach dips? Bad week for the page? The store keeps running. (Followers but no web presence at all? [Read this](/blog/facebook-page-vs-website).)

